Lexical scope means JavaScript determines variable accessibility based on where functions and variables are declared in the source code.

JavaScript maintains lexical environments containing variable bindings and references to their outer environments. When accessing a variable, JavaScript searches the current environment and then follows the scope chain.

A closure is created when a function retains access to its surrounding lexical environment, even after the outer function has completed execution.

For example, we can use closures to implement data privacy, function factories, event handlers and module patterns.

In React, closures are also important because event handlers and callbacks retain values from the render in which they were created. This can sometimes lead to stale closure issues.

Closures are useful for encapsulation, maintaining private state and creating reusable functions.